      "math_academy_id": "q-332822",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+2{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}=2{X}_{1}(s)+{{field-2}} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1={X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)+{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)+2{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+3\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+3L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,L\\{{e}^{-t}\\}=\\frac{1}{s+1},\n$$\n\nwe can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)=2{X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1={X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{3}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
